<section><h2><span>01</span>The goal</h2><p>I saw a video about Strata as an optimized runner for Flash-Next and wondered whether it could improve my local AI setup—and maybe make it a bit faster. So I compared Qwen3.8-27B on llama.cpp (my previous setup) with Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on Strata.</p></section><section><h2><span>02</span>The workstation</h2><p>Same Linux workstation throughout; 128K context, one active request. 27B: Q4_K_M/f16 KV. Flash-Next: IQ3_S/int8 KV.</p><figure><div class="hardware" role="group" aria-label="Workstation and GPU slot connections"><div class="host"><strong>Ryzen 9 9950X</strong><br><span>96 GB RAM</span></div><div class="gpus"><div class="gpu"><h3>GPU 0 · RTX 5060 Ti · 16 GB</h3><p>PCIe 5 ×8<br><b>Fast slot</b></p></div><div class="gpu"><h3>GPU 1 · RTX 5060 Ti · 16 GB</h3><p>Borrowed from my personal PC</p><p>PCIe 3 ×1<br><b>Slow slot</b></p></div></div></div><figcaption>CPU, RAM and GPU work together. Our one-GPU run used ~47 GiB of host memory, including reclaimable mapped weights. Strata 0.1.47 / release v0.2.2 · llama.cpp b11476.</figcaption></figure><div class="experiments"><div><b>Test 1 · Which model?</b><span>Both GPUs active</span><small>27B / llama.cpp vs Flash-Next / Strata</small></div><div><b>Test 2 · How many GPUs?</b><span>Flash-Next: GPU 0 only vs both</span><small>Fresh two-GPU control; same speed probes</small></div></div></section><section><h2><span>03</span>How I tested</h2><p>Same prompts: 87 objective questions, mostly multiple-choice law quizzes, plus five fictional review tasks graded by one AI reviewer with model labels masked.</p><div class="mix" role="img" aria-label="87 objective questions: 60 multiple-choice law quizzes, 24 synthetic workflows, 3 retrieval probes"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><div class="mix-labels"><span><b>60</b> law quizzes</span><span><b>24</b> workflows</span><span><b>3</b> retrieval probes</span></div><p class="context">Text only; sources inline, no attachments or web search. Quizzes used learned knowledge. Speed: three warm runs per input length, thinking off, exactly 128 output tokens, no prompt-cache reuse.</p></section><section><h2><span>04</span>What changed</h2><p>With two GPUs, Flash-Next wrote 3.7–6.1× faster than 27B. Full 128-token replies were 1.5–2.4× faster. Four completed review tasks were 6.3–12.7× faster, with different answer lengths.</p><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:560px)" srcset="/assets/benchmark/gpu-results-mobile.svg"><img src="/assets/benchmark/gpu-results.svg" alt="Flash-Next at 84, 6586 and 26087 input tokens: one GPU writes at 43.6, 64.3 and 61.4 tokens/s; two GPUs at 52.1, 74.1 and 72.9. Full replies take 3.8, 8.8 and 23.5 seconds on one GPU, versus 4.6, 15.5 and 33.2 on two."></picture><figcaption>Test 2 · Flash-Next, one vs two GPUs. Medians of three runs per input length; every answer capped at 128 tokens. Left/top: writing speed. Right/bottom: input processing plus writing. <a href="/evidence/qwen-strata-2026-10-22/gpu-comparison/">All three input lengths and raw GPU follow-up results</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>One GPU reached 43.6 / 64.3 / 61.4 tokens/s across the three input lengths: 13–16% below two GPUs, but full replies arrived 18–43% sooner.</p><aside class="speed-explainer"><b>Why faster writing can still mean a longer wait</b>Tokens are small text chunks. TPS measures writing speed after reading your input. Full reply time = read input + write answer. We fixed answers at 128 tokens. At 26K input, one GPU spent roughly 21.4s reading + 2.1s writing; two spent 31.4s + 1.8s.</aside><table aria-label="Small-sample quality results"><thead><tr><th>Original two-GPU quality</th><th>27B</th><th>Flash-Next</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law quiz · correct choice</td><td>47 / 60</td><td>51 / 60</td></tr><tr><td>Workflow content · audited</td><td>24 / 24</td><td>24 / 24</td></tr><tr><td>Five masked reviews · points</td><td>42 / 50</td><td>47 / 50</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="note">Law uses the separate answer-choice audit. Review points come from one AI reviewer. Workflow content treats [B] and B as the same source; original exact-field grades remain in the report.</p><p>These quizzes and synthetic reviews don’t establish real-work accuracy; public questions may have appeared in training.</p></section><section><h2><span>05</span>Conclusion</h2><p>One fast-slot 5060 Ti was sufficient for these short replies. I’d use one card here. The second card’s slow bus may matter, but its effect wasn’t isolated. Next: longer reasoning answers, realistic document jobs, single-GPU accuracy and a faster second slot.</p></section>